  "prediction": "The report provides a comprehensive list of resources for understanding the legislative process in the United States Congress. The resources include both print and digital materials produced by various government agencies, such as the Government Publishing Office (GPO) and the Congressional Research Service (CRS), as well as commercial publishers.\n\nThe report begins by listing several key publications for understanding the legislative process, including \"Congressional Quarterly's Guide to the U.S. Congress,\" \"Davidson's Congressional Handbook,\" and \"Congressional Quarterly's American Governmental Series.\" These resources cover various aspects of the legislative process, from the structure of Congress to the budgeting process and the role of committees.\n\nThe report also highlights several CRS publications that provide in-depth analysis of specific aspects of the legislative process, such as the role of the House and Senate Parliamentarians, the legislative calendar, and the budget process. These resources are available to congressional offices and can be obtained through the CRS website or by contacting the CRS directly.\n\nAdditionally, the report lists several videos and glossaries that provide visual and interactive explanations of the legislative process. These resources are available to the public through Congress.gov and the CRS website.\n\nFinally, the report notes that some of the resources listed in the report are available for purchase from commercial publishers or bookstores, while others are only available to congressional offices through the House and Senate Parliamentarians or the CRS. The report concludes by encouraging readers to explore these resources to deepen their understanding of the legislative process in the United States Congress.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides a comprehensive list of resources for understanding the legislative process in the United States Congress. The resources include both print and digital materials produced by various government agencies, such as the Government Publishing Office (GPO) and the Congressional Research Service (CRS), as well as commercial publishers.\n\nThe report begins by listing several key publications for understanding the legislative process, including \"Congressional Quarterly's Guide to the U.S. Congress,\" \"Davidson's Congressional Handbook,\" and \"Congressional Quarterly's American Governmental Series.\" These resources cover various aspects of the legislative process, from the structure of Congress to the budgeting process and the role of committees.\n\nThe report also highlights several CRS publications that provide in-depth analysis of specific aspects of the legislative process, such as the role of the House and Senate Parliamentarians, the legislative calendar, and the budget process. These resources are available to congressional offices and can be obtained through the CRS website or by contacting the CRS directly.\n\nAdditionally, the report lists several videos and glossaries that provide visual and interactive explanations of the legislative process. These resources are available to the public through Congress.gov and the CRS website.\n\nFinally, the report notes that some of the resources listed in the report are available for purchase from commercial publishers or bookstores, while others are only available to congressional offices through the House and Senate Parliamentarians or the CRS. The report concludes by encouraging readers to explore these resources to deepen their understanding of the legislative process in the United States Congress.",
  "score": 20.731706924449735,
  "answers": [
    "Written for congressional staff, this report identifies and provides details on how to obtain information on legislative procedures and process in the House and Senate. It provides references to selected CRS products and offers information on the CRS legislative institutes. A listing of selected supplementary materials is also provided. This report will be updated as new information is available."
